{{smaller block|The European Continent is to-day protesting against being ''Teutonized'', as energetically as it did, at the beginning of this century, against a forced conformity to a Gallic organization.
{{right|''G. P. Marsh'', Lects. on Eng. Lang., Int., p. 8.}}}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To conform to German customs, idioms, etc.

'''tew'''<sup>1</sup> (tū), ''v.'' [Also ''tue''; < ME. ''tewen'', a var. of ''tawen'', E. ''taw'': see ''taw''<sup>1</sup>.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To beat, mix, or pound; prepare by beating, etc. [Provincial or trade use.]—'''2.''' To taw, as leather. ''Wright.'' [Prov. Eng.]—'''3.''' To work; prepare by working; be actively employed in or about. [Prov. Eng.]—'''4†.''' To scourge; beat; drub.

{{smaller block|Down with 'em!
Into the wood, and rifle 'em, ''tew'' 'em, swinge 'em!
{{right|''Fletcher'', Beggars' Bush, iii. 2.}}}}

'''5†.''' To haul; pull; tow.

{{smaller block|Men are labouring as 'twere summer bees,
Some hollowing trunks, some binding heaps of wood, . . .
Which o'er the current they by strength must ''tew'';
To shed that blood which many an age shall rue.
{{right|''Drayton'', Barons' Wars, ii. 20.}}}}

'''6.''' To lead on; work up.

{{smaller block|H'as made the gayest sport with Tom the coachman,
So ''tew'd'' him up with sack that he lies lashing
A butt of malmsey for his mares!
{{right|''Fletcher'', Wit without Money, iii. 1.}}}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To work; keep busy; bustle. Also ''too.'' [Prov. Eng. and U. S.]

{{smaller block|The phrase ''tooin''' round, meaning a supererogatory activity like that of flies. ''Lowell'', Biglow Papers, 2d ser., Int.}}

{{smaller block|The minister began to come out of his study, and want to ''tew'' 'round and see to things.}}

