¶
{{smaller block|No more; the ''text'' is foolish. {{right|''Shak.'', Lear, iv. 2. 37.}}}}
{{smaller block|The maiden Aunt
Took this fair day for ''text'', and from it preach'd
An universal culture for the crowd.
{{right|''Tennyson'', Princess, Prol.}}}}
4. In ''vocal music'', the words sung, or to be sung.—5. The main body of matter in a book or manuscript, in distinction from notes or other matter associated with it; by extension, letter-press or reading-matter in general, in distinction from illustrations, or from blank spaces or margins: as, an island of ''text'' in an ocean of margin.
{{smaller block|If the volume is composed of single leaves, perhaps of thin ''text'' and heavy illustrations.
{{right|''W. Matthews'', Modern Bookbinding (ed. Grolier Club), p. 24.}}}}
6. A kind of writing used in the text or body of clerkly manuscripts; formal handwriting; now, especially, a writing or type of a form peculiar to some class of old manuscripts; specifically, in ''her.'', Old English black-letter: as, German or English ''text;'' a ''text'' (black-letter) R or T. {{smaller|An Old English letter often occurs as a bearing or part of a bearing, and is blazoned as above. See also ''black-letter''. Compare ''church text'' and ''German text''.}}
{{smaller block|Fair as a ''text'' B in a copy-book.
{{right|''Shak.'', L. L. L., v. 2. 42.}}}}
'''Chapel text.''' {{smaller|See ''chapel''.}}—'''Church text.''' {{smaller|See ''church''.}}—'''German text.''' {{smaller|See ''German''<sup>2</sup>.}}—'''To cap texts.''' {{smaller|See ''cap''<sup>1</sup>.}}

'''text†''' (tekst), ''v. t.'' [< ''text'', ''n.''] To write in text-hand or large characters.
{{smaller block|Truth copied from my heart is ''texted'' there.
{{right|''Middleton and Dekker'', Spanish Gypsy, iii. 3.}}}}

